I've completed the investigation. Here are my verdicts:

```json
[
  {
    "findingId": "F1",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Every mechanical claim checks out. `MochiSseConfig` (types.ts L250-253) carries only `__mochiSse` and `handler` — there is no `rateLimit` field, so the route cannot declare one. I traced the limiter wiring in Mochi.ts: `resolveLimiter()` is called in exactly two places, the `isMochiPage` branch (L889) and the `isMochiApi` branch (L1198), and `checkRouteLimit()` only at L981 and L1211. Because `buildLimiter()` — the only path that instantiates the serve-level `options.rateLimit` into `sharedGlobalLimiter` — is reached solely through `resolveLimiter()`, a global `rateLimit` genuinely would not cover SSE either; and packages/site/src/index.ts sets none. The SSE registration path (L1336-1433) never consults `routeLimiters`, and `KIND_POLICY.sse.timeout === true` causes `server.timeout(req, 0)` in requestSetup.ts L85-87, so Bun's per-request idle timeout is explicitly disabled for these streams. The site's `protection` gate only matches `/demos/protection*`, CSRF is off for `sse` kind, and the page route's limiter is `skip`-scoped to POST, so an unauthenticated client can open arbitrarily many `GET /demos/queue/events` streams (the path is not slash-mirrored for SSE, so the exact URL matches). Each one appends three handlers to the process-global mitt emitter plus a retained ReadableStream, and `push` calls `queueStatus()` (array copy + reverse) and `JSON.stringify` once per connection per event; one enqueue emits `queue:added` and `queue:completed`, so the cost is 2N. Per spec, `controller.enqueue` appends regardless of `desiredSize`, so a non-reading client also grows its stream's internal queue. Concrete attack: hold N streams from a script (no browser 6-per-host limit), then spend the 60/min POST quota — server work scales linearly in N with nothing in the app or framework to bound N. MEDIUM is appropriate for an unauthenticated, uncapped streaming endpoint with O(N) fan-out on a deployed site."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F2",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "Two of the three links in this chain are confirmed, but the premise is not. Confirmed: `stream.send` reaches a bare `controller.enqueue(frame)` (Mochi.ts L1399) with no try/catch; the only deregistration path is the ReadableStream `cancel()` callback (L1417-1423) — Mochi never registers a `req.signal` abort listener; and mitt's emit really is `i.slice().map(h => h(t))` with no per-handler isolation (verified in node_modules/mitt/dist/mitt.js), while `notifySafely` (queue.ts L544-550) catches only at the whole-emit level, so a throw would silently abort delivery to every later-registered handler. What is missing is any actor that can leave a live `push` bound to a non-readable controller. Per the Streams spec, `ReadableStreamCancel` sets state to `closed` and then synchronously invokes the underlying source's cancel steps, so Mochi's `closeCallbacks` (the three `mochiEvents.off` calls) run in the same step the controller stops accepting chunks — there is no gap. The finding's alternative, 'cancel() has not yet run after the socket closed', is harmless for the opposite reason: until cancel runs the stream is still `readable`, so `enqueue` buffers instead of throwing. Its other alternative, 'a stream errored rather than cancelled', names no erroring party: this demo never calls `stream.close()` or `controller.error()`, and Bun's documented contract is that it calls the stream's `cancel()` when the client disconnects — the historical cancel-not-called bug (oven-sh/bun#8334, #14390) was fixed in Bun 1.1.27, and this repo pins 1.4.4 (.bun-version). Circumstantial support: `/sse/time` and `/sse/admin/stock` use the same blessed idiom (a `setInterval` calling `stream.send`, cleared only in `onClose`), so if `enqueue` threw on a dead controller those long-lived demos would raise an uncaught error within a second of every disconnect. The defensive try/catch plus self-deregistration is still worthwhile hardening, and the recommendation to guard `enqueue` inside the framework is sound, but no reachable failure scenario exists on the pinned runtime."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "The code side is fully verified. The handler sends nothing on open and only reacts to `queue:*` events, so a visitor who reads the page without enqueuing receives zero body bytes; `MochiSseStream.send` emits only `id:`/`event:`/`data:` lines, so the framework has no comment-heartbeat or `retry:` facility; and `KIND_POLICY.sse.timeout` drives `server.timeout(req, 0)`, so Bun will not time the request out either. The upstream premise rests on the project's own attestation at packages/site/src/index.ts L168-170 ('`idleTimeout` keeps Bun's keepalive pings landing inside nginx's 60s `proxy_read_timeout`, which would otherwise drop every idle tab') — nginx's `proxy_read_timeout` is a between-successive-reads timer, so a stream that emits nothing for 60s is exactly what it kills. The client is a plain `EventSource` (QueueWidget.svelte L22) with no `retry:` hint, so the browser auto-reconnects after its default delay, giving continuous ~60s connect/drop churn on an idle viewer plus a few seconds per cycle in which queue events are missed permanently (after hydration the stream is the only source of counter updates). Two caveats temper it: the nginx configuration is not in the repo, so that premise is author-attested rather than verified; and the 'compounding the leak' sub-claim does not hold — the proxy closing the upstream socket is a client abort from Bun's perspective, so `cancel()` fires and the three listeners are removed on each cycle. Net: a real robustness defect with low user-visible impact, which is what BUG already denotes. The suggested fix (initial snapshot plus a 20-30s keep-alive cleared in the existing `onClose`) matches what `/sse/admin/stock` gets for free by pushing every 1.5s."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F4",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The arithmetic and the mechanism both hold. `pendingJobs()` (L18-24) evicts any front entry older than 60s, and the array is a positional FIFO with no job identity, while `settle` (L31-35) does an unconditional `pendingSince.shift()`. I confirmed the throughput assumption against the backend: `concurrency: 2` maps to bun-boss `localConcurrency: 2` with `batchSize: 1` (queue.ts L1086-1089), and bun-boss's `burstWhileNonEmpty` defaults on (manager.ts, resolveInterval) so a backlog drains at fetch rate with no poll gap — with `Bun.sleep(500 + Math.random()*1500)` that is ~1.6 jobs/s, i.e. ~62s (worst case 100s) to drain the 100-job cap, which exceeds PENDING_TTL_MS. Past a backlog of ~96 the oldest entries age out while their jobs are still legitimately queued; each such eviction decrements the count once, and the job's later completion shifts a newer, still-valid entry, so the undercount compounds. Because `reserveNotificationSlot()` (L59) gates admission on the same counter, MAX_PENDING_NOTIFICATION_JOBS stops being a real bound under sustained overload and the in-memory job table grows. The comment at L13-14 that justifies the TTL ('Jobs here take under two seconds') genuinely conflates processing time with queue residency, which is the root cause. Reachability is as described: one IP is capped at 60 POST/min (1/s, below the 1.6/s drain), so it needs a couple of distinct clients, and the state self-heals on full drain since `shift()` on an empty array is a no-op. I also confirmed the existing test (queue.server.test.ts L22-36) only emits a single `queue:added` and fast-forwards `Date.now`, so it cannot catch this. The recommended `Map<jobId, enqueuedAt>` is the right fix — the payloads already carry `jobId` (events.ts MochiQueueAddedEvent/CompletedEvent/FailedEvent)."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F5",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The framework-level claim is exactly right. `makeHandler` emits `queue:failed` from its catch on every attempt, carrying `attempt: raw.retryCount + 1` (queue.ts L576), whereas `queue:added` is emitted only from the producer methods (L598, L617), never on a retry. bun-boss's `failJobsBody` retries while `retry_count < retry_limit` (manager.ts ~L1513 / plans.ts failJobsBody), and the stored default is `retry_limit: 2` (plans.ts QUEUE_DEFAULTS L61) — Mochi mirrors that default and this descriptor declares no `retryLimit`, so a job failing every attempt produces one `queue:added` and three `queue:failed`, i.e. one push and three shifts, retiring two other in-flight jobs' entries. So the one-in/one-out assumption encoded in `settle` (subscribed to both `queue:completed` and `queue:failed`, L36-37) is unsound, and it also decrements on a non-terminal failure while the job is still pending. I agree with the reporter's own scoping that this is latent rather than live: the processor (L43-53) does `Bun.sleep`, an array push/shift and an increment, with `job.data.user` always populated by `enqueueNotification`, so there is no throw path, and expiry-based failures are raised by bun-boss's supervisor rather than by `makeHandler`, so they emit no Mochi event. That matters for prioritisation, but the defect is real in code that files.ts publishes verbatim as demo/template source, where it will be copied into processors that do throw. Distinct from F4: different trigger (event multiplicity vs TTL-vs-residency) and separately fixable, even though the suggested jobId-keyed Map happens to cure both."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F6",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The leak itself is verified end to end. Both token pages take the token from the query string (L88, L110) and render through the normal shell; packages/support/src/shell.html contains `{{mochi.analytics}}`; `analytics({ exclude: [NEWSLETTER_EMBED_PATH] })` (index.ts L41) excludes only `/newsletter/embed`, and packages/shared/src/index.ts injects the tracker for any non-excluded path whenever `MOCHI_DOCKER === 'true'` — which Dockerfile.production sets (L75), the image used to deploy this package. analyticsTracker.html sets only `data-website-id` and `data-performance`, not `data-exclude-search`, and Umami's documented behaviour is to collect the query string unless that attribute is set (it was added in v2.11.0), so the full token-bearing URL is POSTed to u.khromov.se and persisted there. I also confirmed `unsubscribe_token` is never rotated — only `subInsertStmt` ever writes it; `subReArmStmt` refreshes `confirm_token` but not the unsubscribe one — and that TokenPage.svelte already carries `<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex\">`, so the exposure class was recognised and this channel simply missed. One correction to the impact narrative: the pageview that logs a token is the same request that performs the action, so a logged `confirm_token` is already spent (a replay hits the `status === 'confirmed'` branch and returns 'already'), and a logged `unsubscribe_token` has already been used. The residual capability is narrower than stated — the never-rotated unsubscribe token can re-unsubscribe anyone who later re-subscribes, and any future flow change (or a prefetch-then-log ordering) re-opens the rest. I keep MEDIUM because writing live capability tokens into an off-origin analytics store with its own access model is the finding class (CWE-598/532) and the fixes offered — adding both paths to `exclude`, `data-exclude-search`, and redirecting to a bare result page — are all cheap and correct."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F7",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The asymmetry is real and sits directly against the invariant the code documents for itself at db.server.ts L257-259. The HTTP responses are indistinguishable — `already` and `created` both fall through to the bare `success()` at L76 — but L66 gates `appendNewsletterLog()` and the awaited `newsletterEmailQueue.add()` on `outcome.kind !== 'already'`, so an address that is a confirmed subscriber short-circuits both. The delta is larger than it first looks: the log insert commits on support.sqlite, and the queue insert commits on a separate SQLite file that Mochi opens with no WAL/synchronous pragma (queue.ts L494, and bun-boss's adapter sets only `foreign_keys` and `busy_timeout`), so the non-`already` path pays extra fsyncing commits plus serialization behind the worker's in-flight queries — a millisecond-scale mean shift, not microseconds. The sampling model also holds up: `requestSubscription` checks the cooldown ahead of the confirmed branch (so the response shape stays uniform), which leaves one clean sample per address per RESEND_COOLDOWN_MS, about 12/hour, comfortably inside the 20-POST/10-min limit and its 3-strike ban, at 19 bits of PoW per probe (DEFAULT_CAPTCHA_BITS) — and an attacker-owned control address exercises the non-`already` path on every probe, cancelling network drift. Probing a confirmed address is side-effect-free as claimed. Honest caveats: the signal must be recovered from internet jitter, so hundreds of interleaved samples per address are needed, and every probe of a non-subscriber mails that address a confirmation, so the campaign is noisy. It stays a true positive because nothing in the code closes the channel, and the suggested fix (always log and enqueue, letting jobs.server.ts L35's `status !== 'pending'` guard no-op the `already` case, or normalise to a fixed response floor) is cheap."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F8",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "Every link in the chain verifies. `claim()` is `UPDATE … SET email_status='sending' WHERE id=? AND email_status='pending'` (db.server.ts L136), and the only releases are `noteAttemptError` (reachable solely from the inner catch when `Mochi.email` throws) and `requeue` (admin resend via `refreshConfirmToken`, L343) — abnormal termination is neither. packages/support/src/index.ts sets no `queueShutdownTimeout`, so `mountQueues` applies DEFAULT_QUEUE_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT = 10_000 (queue.ts L229, L1065), and the framework's own test asserts the consequence: after the budget expires the job is left not-completed (queueWorker.test.ts L243-280). bun-boss then recovers it — `supervise` defaults true at a 60s interval (attorney.ts L387, L642) and `failJobsByTimeout` re-inserts an `active` job past `started_on + expire_seconds` as `retry` while `retry_count < retry_limit` (plans.ts L1743, failJobsBody); this queue leaves `expireInSeconds` at the 900s default. The retry then reaches L41, `claimNewsletterSend` returns false because the row is still 'sending', and the processor logs `{ event: 'sent', detail: 'Skipped — another job already handled this confirmation' }` and resolves, so bun-boss completes the job and never retries: the confirmation is silently never delivered and the log labels the loss 'sent'. I am downgrading from HIGH_BUG for three reasons the write-up misses or overstates. The trigger needs an abnormal termination inside the seconds-wide claim→markSent window (SIGKILL/OOM/crash, or a SIGTERM with a send outlasting 10s — 'routinely exceeds 10s' overstates a typical SMTP delivery). It is not undiagnosable: EmailLogDialog renders the row's `email_status`, so the subscriber sits visibly at 'email sending', and the log detail says 'Skipped'. And there is a third release path the finding does not mention — a user re-submitting after the cooldown hits `subReArmStmt`, which resets `email_status='pending'` with a fresh token and enqueues a new job, so the affected person recovers by trying again. The stale-claim fix (a `claimed_at` staleness window) and the distinct 'skipped' log event are both still the right changes."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F9",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Both halves are straightforwardly correct. `Number('0')` is 0 and therefore falsy, so `NEWSLETTER_RESEND_COOLDOWN_MS=0` and `NEWSLETTER_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=0` are discarded in favour of the 5-minute / 14-day defaults — an operator who tries to disable the resend cooldown in staging gets silently ignored, and both knobs are documented, operator-facing variables in packages/support/.env.example (lines 64 and 66), so this is a supported configuration surface rather than a hypothetical. A negative value is truthy and passes through: with `NEWSLETTER_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=-1`, `requestSubscription` stores `confirm_expires_at = now + negative` (db.server.ts L267), the confirm route's `confirm_expires_at <= Date.now()` check (routes.ts L98) returns 'expired' on the recipient's first click, and `purgeExpiredPendingSubscribers` — which selects `status='pending' AND confirm_expires_at < ?` (db.server.ts L241) — deletes every new signup on the next nightly cron run. Signup breaks completely and silently, and index.ts's `mochi:init` hook validates only ADMIN_PASSWORD, SMTP_HOST and MOCHI_ORIGIN, so there is no boot-time guard. The house-pattern argument also checks out verbatim: adminAuth.ts L31-34 carries exactly the quoted comment and uses `Number.isFinite(configured) && configured >= 0`. This is operator-triggered rather than attacker-triggered, which is what BUG severity already reflects; the recommended `Number.isFinite && >= 0` parse (or a boot-time throw on a negative TTL) is the correct fix."
  }
]
```

**Sources** for the external-behaviour checks: [Umami tracker configuration](https://docs.umami.is/docs/tracker-configuration), [Umami v2.11.0 release](https://www.github.com/umami-software/umami/releases/tag/v2.11.0), [Bun SSE guide](https://bun.com/guides/http/sse), [oven-sh/bun#8334 — `Bun.serve` never closes `ReadableStream` on cancellation](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/8334), [oven-sh/bun#14390 — ReadableStream cancel is not called](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/14390), [Bun v1.1.27 release notes](https://bun.sh/blog/bun-v1.1.27).
